**Linear** $y=mx+c$ is a straight line with a constant slope.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-2.8,0) -- (2.8,0);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-2.6) -- (0,2.8);
\node at (3.05,-0.3) {$x$};
\node at (-0.35,3.0) {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,<->] (-2.2,-1.6) -- (2.2,2.2);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

**Quadratic** $y=ax^2+bx+c$, where $a\ne0$, is a parabola with a turning point and line symmetry.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-2.8,0) -- (2.8,0);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-2.6) -- (0,2.8);
\node at (3.05,-0.3) {$x$};
\node at (-0.35,3.0) {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,<->,domain=-2.1:2.1,smooth,samples=60,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.8*\x*\x-1.3});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

**Exponential** $y=a^x$, where $a>0$ and $a\ne1$, is a curve that flattens toward an asymptote on one side and shoots away on the other.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-2.8,0) -- (2.8,0);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-2.6) -- (0,2.8);
\node at (3.05,-0.3) {$x$};
\node at (-0.35,3.0) {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,<->,domain=-2.2:1.4,smooth,samples=80,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{exp(\x*ln(2))});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

